## Introduction
Noble metal nanoclusters [1,2], characterized by ultra-small size, discrete electron energy levels, and unique molecular-like features [3],have been a hot research spot that draws researchers attention because of their particular luminescent [4, 5], catalytic [6,7], chiral [8],magnetic [9], and electrochemical properties [10]. Among the large number of luminescent noble metal nanoclusters, silver nanoclusters $(\mathrm{{Ag}\mathrm{{NCs})}}$ are well known for their many advantages such as unique electronic states and optical properties [ll-15], which have been potentially useful candidates for applications in various fields such as fluorescence sensor and catalysis. Hence, the synthesis of silver nanocluster with delocalized electrons has been the focus of research interest [l6-l8]. However, contrary to common nanomaterials like mesoporous silica [19] and graphene [20],the $\mathrm{Ag}\ \mathrm{NCs}$ with delocalized electrons are confronted with that situation of easy oxidation or instability, which greatly impedes their further application. They are sensitive to environment, easy to decompose or aggregate affected by some external factors such as light conditions, temperatures, and $\mathrm{pH},$ and satisfactory applications can not be obtained in luminescence and catalysis [2l-23].Therefore, the rational synthesis of stable Ag NCs remains a challenge.
Generally, to deal with this dilemma, small Ag NCs can be confined in zeolites [24,25],metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) [26-28],or other systems [29, 30] based on the confinement effect, which improves their stability to a certain extent. MOF,with the comprehensive superiority of strong designability, high flexibility, and easy modification of structures, has been extensively used as a host material for confining noble metal nanoparticles (NPs) or nanoclusters [3l-38]. In 20l4, based on post-confinement strategy, for the first time, Jiang's group first introduced the metal precursors of $\mathrm{Pd^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Co^{2+}}$ into the mesoporous $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{III})$ Materials Institute Lavoisier, MIL-l0l by the double solvents approach, followed by in-situ reduction, and successfully realized the encapsulation  of  bimetallic  core-shell  nanoparticles by mesoporous MOF under mild conditions [39]. Benefited from the confinement effect of MIL-l0l, the resultant $\operatorname{Pd}\ @\operatorname{Co}$ NPs were superior to their monometallic counterparts and alloy NPs in terms of better economy and easy recycle and effectively avoid the problem of easy aggregation of small NPs into large size NPs. At the same time, the composite Pd@Co@MIL-10l exhibited excellent catalytic activity and cycling stability in hydrolytic dehydrogenation of ammonia borane reaction. In 202l, Fei and coworkers, adopting the method of post-confinement synthesis, successfully  obtained ultrasmall and highly dispersed gold nanoclusters stabilized with N-heterocyclic carbenes (NHCs) ligand in parent University of Oslo, UiO-68-NHC [40]. And the resultant Au-NC@UiO-68-NHC composite not only exhibited high photostability and excellent chemical stability, but also showed a favourable and stable activity in photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, obviously surpassing the control group without NHCligand stabilization. The confined growth of metal nanoclusters in MOFs provides a viable approach for the development of stable clusters and also offers greater possibilities for their potential application. At present, MOFs have been used as the suitable matrix for confining noble metal nanoclusters,where Au clusters are in the majority [33,34,40], and are mainly focused on the catalysis study. Comparatively speaking, it was rarely reported about metal clusters confined by lanthanide MOFs, especially $\mathrm{Ag}$ NCs. The challenge of effective confinement methods and how to take advantage of MOFs to confine nanoclusters still exist.
In the synthesis and study of noble metal nanoclusters, the alkynyl ligand is a relatively new ligand, containing $\pi$ bonds and $\upsigma$ bonds to form a conjugate system of the whole metal cluster, which can combine organic carbon and inorganic metal well so as to obtain a stable configuration [4l,42]. Generally, the reactivity of alkynyl and its derivatives is relatively low because of sphybridization carbon atoms, so the activation of $\mathrm{C}\mathrm{\equiv}\mathrm{C}$ bond is a critical step in their synthesis and transformation. Due to the excellent $\pi$ -Lewis acidity, and excellent affinity for carbon and proton-like properties, silver(I) is peculiarly prone to replace the acidic hydrogen of the terminal alkyne, thus activating the $\mathrm{C}\mathrm{\equiv}\mathrm{C}$ bond via $\upsigma{\cdot}$ -coordination to form silver acetylide [43]. The relatively inexpensive silver reagents are an idealistic option for the activation of $\mathrm{C}\mathrm{\equiv}\mathrm{C}$ bond in comparison with other noble metals. Furthermore, the Ag NCs stabilized by alkynyl ligands possess peculiar electronic structure and optical properties [44]. To the best of our knowledge, there are no reports of confinement of silver clusters by using alkynyl ligands-containing lanthanide MOFs, which opens a door for studying the change of luminescence behaviour triggered by the mutual interactions between guest $\mathrm{AgNCs}$ and host luminescent MOFs.
In this work, we adopted bifunctional ligands, 5-ethynylisophthalic acid (denoted as $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ebdc})$ with dicarboxylic and alkynyl groups to construct lanthanide luminescent MOF materials (denoted as Eu-MOF) by solvothermal method, and the resultant composite Ag NCs@Eu-MOF through a simple post-synthetic modification strategy (Scheme 1). The Eu-MOF shows dual emission properties of both ligands and $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ ions, and good temperature-dependent photoluminescence, which is a good candidate for a ratiometric fluorescent thermometer. The changes of optical properties and thermal stability of host Eu-MOF after introducing silver cluster were specifically discussed, and the study of Ag NCs $@$ Eu-MOF as a heterogeneous catalyst was also briefly explored.
